  "strategy": "Based on the problem formulation and constraints, I propose a new strategy called **Adaptive Fourier-time Domain Optimization (AFDOD)**. This strategy combines the strengths of frequency-domain analysis with time-domain optimization, leveraging the properties of the autocorrelation function and its relationship with the Fourier transform.\n\n### 1. Approach and Rationale\n\nThe objective is to find non-negative sequences that minimize the normalized maximum autocorrelation:\n\n```math\nf = \\min \\quad \\frac{2n \\cdot \\max_k (\\mathit{convolve}(a, a)[k])}{(\\sum a_i)^2}\n```\nThis can be rephrased in terms of the Fourier transform of the sequence. \n\nThe key idea is to:\n1. Restrict frequencies to minimize \\|f*f\\| while avoiding pathological combinations resembling communication signals\n2. Use a graduated coefficient strategy based on frequency weighting instead of simple logarithmic updates\n3. Tailor search effort based on the sequence's intrinsic properties (forming temperature classes)\n\nThe bottleneck in previous methods was their inability to simultaneously control spectral growth and maintain non-negativity constraints. My approach directly addresses this by:\n- Utilizing FFT-based analytic gradients\n- Implementing harmonic damping to prevent abrupt frequency transitions\n- Using sequence separation (harmonic islands) to minimize self-interference\n\n### 2. Mathematical Specification\n\nRepresent the sequence as a discrete signal:\n\n```math\nx[n] \\in \\mathbb{R}^+ \\quad \\text{for sequence generation}\n\\mathbb{y}[k] = \\sum_{i+j=k} x[i] \\cdot x[j] \\quad \\text{linear convolution}\n```\n\nThe optimization objective in the time domain is homogeneous, so we normalize the sequence:\n\n```math\ns = \\sum x_i, \\quad x = \\frac{x}{s}, \\quad s \\geq 0.01\n```\n\nThen, the objectives become:\n```math\n\\min \\quad \\frac{2n \\cdot \\max_k \\mathbb{y}[k]}{1} \\quad \\left(\\text{normalized to unit sum}\\right)\n```\n\nWe formulate this using the Fourier transform properties:\n\n```math\nZ = \\mathit{FFT}(a), \\quad \\mathbb{y} = \\mathit{IFFT}(|Z|^2)\n```\n\nThis transforms the constraints into frequency domain:\n\n```math\n|\\mathbb{y}[k]|_\\infty \\leq M \\iff |\\mathit{convolve}(a, a)|_\\infty \\leq M \\iff |\\mathbb{y}|_\\infty \\leq M\n```\n\n### 3. Implementation Procedure\n\nThe algorithm proceeds through four distinct phases:\n\n```python\n#---Pseudocodeforoverallalgorithm---#\n\nimport numpy as np\nfrom scipy.fft import fft, ifft\n\ndef propose_candidate(seq_parent=None, time_budget=1500):\n    \"\"\"Performssearchfortheminimumautocorrelationstepfunction.\"\"\"\n    \n    # Initializationandnormalization\n    if seq_parent is None:\n        n = 400\n        a = np.ones(n) / n  # Uniformstartingpoint\n    else:\n        a_norm, _ = normalize_sequence(seq_parent)\n        n = len(seq_parent)\n    \n    best_seq = a_norm.copy()\n    best_score = evaluate_sequence(best_seq)\n    time_remaining = time_budget\n    temp_class = \"low\"\n    \n    # Phasetemperaturemappingandtimewindows\n    phase_timewindows = {\n        \"spectral_shape\": 10.0,\n        \"cross_phase\": 5.0,\n        \"polish\": 2.0\n    }\n    \n    # Stage1:Spectralconditioning\n    time_start = time.time()\n    print(\"Phase1: SpectralConditioning\")\n    temp = 0.5  # Initialtemperature\n    \n    # FPSamplingfortimewindows\n    for phase_name, duration in phase_timewindows.items():\n        if time.time() - time_start > time_budget * 0.25: break\n        if duration is None: continue\n        \n        n_new = adapt_length(best_seq)  # Learnsfromcurrentbest\n        if phase_name == \"spectral_shape\":\n            try:\n                # Primarysearch:Fouriertomodetime\n                a_temp = fourier_to_time(best_seq, temp)  # InvertsdefocusedFourierplan\n                new_score = evaluate_sequence(a_temp)\n                if new_score < best_score:\n                    best_score = new_score\n                    best_seq = a_temp.copy()\n            except:\n                fallback = generate_alternate_phase()\n                if fallback: best_seq = fallback / np.sum(fallback)\n                pass\n        \n        # Restatetotheroundedlength\n        if time.time() - time_start > 0.6 * time_budget:\n            a = resample_sequence(best_seq, 10)\n```\n\n### 4. Search Choices and Variations\n\nThe search dynamically selects phases based on candidate properties:\n\n```python\ndef adapt_phase(phases, score_history):\n    \"\"\"Adaptssearchphasetotemporaryminimum.\"\"\"\n    s = sorted(score_history, reverse=True)\n    if score_history[-1] < s[0] * 0.99 or len(score_history) > 100:\n        return phases[\"cool\"]\n    elif len(score_history) % 2 == 0:\n        return phases[\"warm\"]\n    else:\n        return phases[\"mixed\"]\n```\n\n### 5. Compute Budget and Robustness\n\nTime management:\n\n```python\nbudget_allocation = {\n    \"initial\": 0.1,\n    \"shape_search\": 0.4,\n    \"alternation\": 0.4,\n    \"cross_terms\": 0.05\n}\n\nclass TimeBudgetHandler:\n    def __init__(self, budget):\n        self.budget = budget\n        \n    def manage_timewindows(self, current_sequence):\n        \"\"\"AdjustestimewindowsbasedonFPUbehavior.\"\"\"\n        lengths = [len(x) for x in current_sequence]\n        n = max(lengths) if lengths else 1\n        \n        # Sharpenssearchforklargerthannearly\n        if n < 500:\n            reduced_budget = budget * 0.05\n        else:\n            reduced_budget = budget * 0.1\n            \n        # PrioritizesL-BFGS-Bforcomputationallyefficientnodes\n        return {'L-BFGS-B': reduced_budget, 'CVX': 0.03 * budget}\n```\n\nFallback initialization:\n\n```python\ndef generate_alternate_phase(previous_score=None):\n    \"\"\"Generatesfallbackcandidatewithenshuffledcomponents\"\"\"\n    n = 200  # Computationallylightfallbacklength\n    \n    # Selectionbasedonfrequencycontentspreadness\n    waveform = sinedecay(n)\n    waveform = resample_excited_states(waveform, n)\n    \n    # Ensuresuniformfrequencydistribution\n    step = 100\n    \n    # Multi-stepfallbackgenerator\n    for i in range(0, n, step):\n        if i == n//2:\n            last_part = ifft(...)\n        else:\n            last_part = rfftonly(...)\n```\n\n### 6. Validation and Return Contract\n\nFinalsequencevalidation:\n\n```python\n# Callwith_alltobetestedforfinitevalues\ndef converge_to_minimum(confirm_shortoutput):\n    \"\"\"Safeguardsoverallitterationagainstfalseimprovements\"\"\"\n    \n    # Short-circuitifconfirm_shortoutputisavailable\n    if confirm_shortoutput:\n        # Usesanalyticgradientfromscipyoptimize\n        endpoint, final_value = finalize_optimization_phase()\n    else:\n        # Proceedsreservoirsamplinguntil\n        endpoint = generic_search_convergence()\n    \n    # Reconstructsanexamplefromfinaloutput\n    return reconstruct_example(endpoint)\n\ndef reconstruct_example(seq):\n    \"\"\"Normalizesandinstantiatesevalsequencecomponents\"\"\"\n    s = np.sum(seq)\n    seq = seq / s  # Enforcesdenormalizationconstraint\n    \n    # Re-evaluateswithoriginalobjective\n    value_inf = evaluate_sequence(seq)\n    if np.isnan(value_inf) or value_inf == float('inf'):\n        # Fallbackoncomputation\n        return create_artificial_step()\n    \n    return seq\n\ndef create_artificial_step():\n    \"\"\"Generatesfallbacksteplatticeofminimizedpulsarity\"\"\"\n    length = 1000  # Steady-statefallbackpreference\n    \n    # Superpositionofknownminimalpatterns\n    lattice = multiple_shaping()\n    lattice_value = evaluate_sequence(lattice)\n    \n    # Ensuresonlyvalidcandidatesareshipped\n    if lattice_value in (np.inf, float('inf')):\n        return uniform_series_parametric()\n    \n    return lattice_value_scaled()\n\nbase_lattice = create_artificial_step(min_steps=500)\nbase_value = evaluate_sequence(base_lattice)\nif base_value <= valid_threshold:\n    return base_value\n```\n\n Full implementation requires translating this detailed strategy into concrete Python operations. The key innovations are:\n1) Frequency-dependent processing (fourier_to_time)\n2) Dynamic phase adaptation (adapt_phase)\n3) Budget-efficient candidate generation (fourier_to_time variants)\n4) Gradient-harmonized damping (TimeBudgetHandler)\n\nThis approach directly addresses the ordering requirement by exploiting Fourier transform symmetries while arming the optimizer with accurate gradients. All candidate generation and optimization respects the normalization and range constraints enforced by evaluate_sequence.",
